==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
From earlier {{m|enm|mingil}}, {{m|en|mengle}}, from {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|*mengelen}}, equivalent to {{suffix|ming|le|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|mengen||to mingle, mix}}, {{cog|de|mengen||to mingle, mix}}. More at {{l|en|ming}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈmɪŋ.ɡəl/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-mingle.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪŋɡəl|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|mingl}}

# To [[mix]]; [[intermix]]; to [[combine]] or [[join]], as an individual or part, with other parts, but commonly so as to be distinguishable in the product; to [[confuse]]; to [[confound]].
#* Bible, Exodus ix. 24
#*: There was {{...}} fire '''mingled''' with the hail.
#: ''Across the city yesterday, there was a feeling of bittersweet reunion as streams of humanity converged and mingled at dozens of memorial services.'' &mdash; [http://www.nytimes.com/2006/09/11/nyregion/nyregionspecial3/11bush.html?hp&ex=1158033600&en=e468f88da52557ed&ei=5094&partner=homepage New York Times]
# To associate or unite in society or by ties of relationship; to cause or allow to intermarry; to intermarry.
#* Bible, Ezra ix. 2
#*: The holy seed have '''mingled''' themselves with the people of those lands.
# To deprive of purity by mixture; to [[contaminate]].
#* Henry Rogers
#*: a '''mingled''', imperfect virtue
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To put together; to join.
#: {{rfquotek|Shakespeare}}
# To make or prepare by mixing the ingredients of.
#* {{w|Nathaniel Hawthorne}}
#*: [He] proceeded to '''mingle''' another draught.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become mixed or blended.

